title The order a read stopped rebuilding
prompt The baton, and the card's own open door for this seat -- does current() want the same reading? It sorts per call.
rota lap 4238, row 3 -- Water, the row that tastes. Its third seat now reads what-brix-infuse-is: infusion(world') -> world', running the same thing twice moves nothing. That is exactly what current() was doing -- sorting a list already in place order, an operation whose second application is the whole of its first. The threshold page's own discipline, taste only works up close, is why the reading was a measurement rather than an argument.
think Weave.current filtered present lines and then sorted them by place on every call. The question the card left was whether that sort is owed. It is not: the backing list is in place order by construction, held by all three ways a weave comes to exist.
think empty starts with no lines. apply appends its inserts carrying next_run, and its own postcondition already asserts every held line's run stands below next_run -- so an appended line sorts after every held line whatever its site, and the run of them sorts among itself by a position counter that only rises. Deletes change a generation and move nothing. merge sorts its result by place and asserts places strictly increase before returning.
think So the order is established where a line is placed rather than re-established where a line is read. A subsequence of an ordered list is ordered, so the filter carries document order out with it.
think Measured before writing the sentence. ReleaseSafe, twenty reads of a weave holding n present lines, best of three: 16,384 unchanged inside noise; 131,072 3.3ms to 2.7ms; 524,288 25.3ms to 13.3ms. A factor of two at half the ceiling, and nothing at small sizes.
think Said the shape honestly in the module head rather than overselling it. What remains dominates -- the read copies every present line into a caller-owned slice, about sixteen megabytes of Line at 524,288, and no ordering change touches that.
obs The new assert is a restatement and no control phase isolates it. Two plants were built and run: removing merge's place sort reds through merge's own postcondition, and removing that assert too still reds through the field-for-field equality claim, which two differently-ordered merges fail whatever the read does. Both exited 134 with the sort here and without it. The isolating phase was declined rather than written and the decline is recorded in the module head -- a plant that reds for a reason the phase did not name proves the same nothing as a plant that matched no line, which is the fault the merge control already warns about in its own comments.
obs Neither weave control targeted current()'s sort, so removing it broke no plant. The merge control's order phase and the annotate control's order phase both still match uniquely.
obs Reds first. The cold open answered five red and I closed four. shell_dialect was a fresh one an hour old from a peer's lap and is booked as %602. reds_ledger_headline and reds_ledger_monotone were headline drift my own seat's %599 fold left behind. log_has_a_row was a pin count of 125 against a shelf of 128; a peer landed the same repair upstream and my copy dropped cleanly on the rebase, which is the fleet working.
obs stash_record stays red and is not mine. The card names it as Bakery's %592, interlocked -- landing that record reds log_file_claim -- and superseded by a peer's broader rule six.
obs %602 was booked twice. The first booking read next_free=602 off the anointed spine while this tree stood at 599, which opened gaps at 600 and 601 and reddened reds_ledger_monotone at once. Withdrawn, rebased onto xy/main, booked again on the spine the number came from. Fetch-before-book is not enough by itself; the row has to land on the fetched tree.
obs The hot pass reddened three guards on my own staged tree, all three my leavings and all three closed. unshared_citation: the card cited %602 twice while the anointed spine had bound only 601, which is derived-spine rule 4 exactly -- cite the stamp until the row is shared. The card names the stamp now; the ledger row and the dated log are exempt by that law's own reading, one because it assigns and one because it is testimony.
obs equinox_e123_living_pin_guard: the new row carried REDS.md from 40,439 to 42,539 against a bound of 40,960. Every other row on the pin reads OPEN, so the only foldable row was my own, folded on arrival through tools/fixtures/r/reds_fold.sh -- the same shape my seat took last lap with %499. reds_row_present still finds it, which is the point of folding through the tool rather than by hand.
obs fold_shelf_link_repoint: the block I shelved carried card-relative links that went one directory deeper with it. tools/f/fold_shelf_link_repoint.sh --apply made both repairs. Three guards, three faults a hand would have shipped, and each named its own repair in its own output.
obs The hot pass over the staged tree: 183 guards, 177 green, 3 gated, 3 red. index_row_bound was a peer's row order standing at HEAD -- 20260908.005205 above 20260908.010501 on today's shelf -- repaired through the tool that names itself in the refusal, tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh, and proven GREEN after. stash_record stays another seat's. standing_equipment is the roll-up of those two.
obs Two of my own hot passes overlapped and the second refused on the run lock, which is one writer per checkout working exactly as %291 asks. Stopped mine by exact PID rather than by pattern: pgrep at that moment showed two OTHER ships' hot passes in the same process table, which is %569's whole lesson, and a pkill -f would have reached both.
obs My ledger row withdrew whole on the rebase. A peer published the identical sed -i cure first, and the ledger already carries the lesson twice -- %553 for a bare sed -i in a control, %591 for a heredoc body read as program text. A third telling adds nothing, so the row, its shelf and its recital line all came out and the peer's repair stands. Eleventh collision in two days, and the third where two ships found one FAULT rather than one number.
obs What the booking taught before it went is worth keeping and is why the card still carries it: fetch-before-book is not enough by itself. The first booking read next_free off the anointed spine while this tree stood three rows behind it, which opened gaps at 600 and 601 and reddened reds_ledger_monotone at once. The row has to land on the fetched tree, not merely take a number read from it.
